ps:TroubleshootingGuide a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "Troubleshooting Guide: Common Issues and Solutions" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/troubleshooting/GUIDE.md" ;
    ps:phase 2 ;
    ps:priority "P1" ;
    ps:targetPersona "developer" ;
    ps:wordCount 2600 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "15 minutes" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S1, ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S2, ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S3, ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S4 .

ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "RDF Syntax Errors" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Error**: "Expected '.', found ';'"
**Cause**: Incorrect Turtle syntax terminator
**Solution**: Use `;` to continue with same subject, `.` to end statement
```turtle
# Wrong
a2a:AgentCard a a2a:Entity .
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:agentId ;

# Right
a2a:AgentCard a a2a:Entity ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:agentId .
```

**Error**: "Undefined prefix 'ps'"
**Cause**: Missing @prefix declaration
**Solution**: Add prefix at top of file
```turtle
@prefix ps: <https://ggen.io/ontology/paradigm-shift/> .
```

**Error**: "Invalid IRI"
**Cause**: Spaces or special characters in IRI
**Solution**: Use proper IRI encoding or wrap in < >
```turtle
# Wrong
ps:Mental Model Shift a ps:Document .

# Right
ps:MentalModelShift a ps:Document .
# or
<http://example.org/Mental Model Shift> a ps:Document .
```

**Error**: "Literal not allowed as subject"
**Cause**: Used string literal where IRI expected
**Solution**: Literals can only be objects, not subjects/predicates
```turtle
# Wrong
"AgentCard" a a2a:Entity .

# Right
a2a:AgentCard a a2a:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "AgentCard" .
```

**Debugging workflow**:
1. Run turtle validator: `rapper -i turtle file.ttl`
2. Check line number in error message
3. Verify prefix declarations
4. Check punctuation (. vs ; vs ,)
5. Validate IRIs are properly formatted
""" .

ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "SPARQL Query Issues" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Error**: "CONSTRUCT query returns empty graph"
**Cause**: WHERE pattern doesn't match any data
**Solution**: Debug with SELECT first
```sparql
# Debug version
SELECT ?entity ?prop WHERE {
    ?entity a a2a:Entity ;
        a2a:hasProperty ?prop .
}

# If SELECT returns rows, CONSTRUCT should work
CONSTRUCT {
    ?entity a a2a:GeneratedStruct ;
        a2a:hasField ?prop .
} WHERE {
    ?entity a a2a:Entity ;
        a2a:hasProperty ?prop .
}
```

**Error**: "Variable not bound in CONSTRUCT"
**Cause**: CONSTRUCT uses variable not present in WHERE
**Solution**: Ensure all CONSTRUCT variables appear in WHERE
```sparql
# Wrong - ?foo not in WHERE
CONSTRUCT { ?entity a2a:hasField ?foo } WHERE { ?entity a a2a:Entity }

# Right
CONSTRUCT { ?entity a2a:hasField ?prop } WHERE {
    ?entity a a2a:Entity ; a2a:hasProperty ?prop
}
```

**Error**: "ORDER BY variable not in scope"
**Cause**: Ordering by variable not projected
**Solution**: Include ordering variable in query
```sparql
# Wrong
CONSTRUCT { ?doc ps:title ?title } WHERE { ?doc ps:title ?title }
ORDER BY ?order  # ?order not selected

# Right
CONSTRUCT { ?doc ps:title ?title ; ps:order ?order } WHERE {
    ?doc ps:title ?title ; ps:order ?order
}
ORDER BY ?order
```

**Performance issue**: "Query takes minutes to execute"
**Cause**: Cartesian product from unconnected patterns
**Solution**: Ensure patterns are joined via shared variables
```sparql
# Slow - disconnected patterns
WHERE { ?x a Type1 . ?y a Type2 }  # Every x × every y

# Fast - connected via shared variable
WHERE { ?x a Type1 ; rel:linkTo ?y . ?y a Type2 }
```

**Debugging workflow**:
1. Replace CONSTRUCT with SELECT * to see raw data
2. Add one pattern at a time to WHERE clause
3. Check each variable is bound
4. Verify ORDER BY variables are projected
5. Use LIMIT 10 during debugging
""" .

ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Template Rendering Issues" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Error**: "Template variable 'foo' not found"
**Cause**: CONSTRUCT didn't produce expected property
**Solution**: Check CONSTRUCT output matches template expectations
```tera
{# Template expects #}
{{ doc.title }}

{# CONSTRUCT must produce #}
CONSTRUCT { ?doc ps:docTitle ?title }
WHERE { ?doc ps:title ?title }

{# Template context binding #}
documents: [{ title: "..." }]
```

**Error**: "Unexpected type: wanted String, got Array"
**Cause**: RDF property has multiple values (1-to-many)
**Solution**: Iterate over array or use LIMIT 1 in CONSTRUCT
```tera
{# Wrong - assumes single value #}
{{ doc.author }}

{# Right - handle array #}
{% for author in doc.authors %}{{ author }}{% endfor %}

{# Or in CONSTRUCT, enforce single value #}
WHERE { ?doc ps:author ?author } LIMIT 1
```

**Error**: "Division by zero" in template
**Cause**: Missing data causes arithmetic error
**Solution**: Use default filter or check existence
```tera
{# Wrong #}
{{ 100 / doc.total }}

{# Right #}
{{ 100 / doc.total | default(value=1) }}
{# or #}
{% if doc.total %}{{ 100 / doc.total }}{% endif %}
```

**Error**: "Rendered output has wrong indentation/formatting"
**Cause**: Template whitespace control
**Solution**: Use `{%-` and `-%}` for whitespace trimming
```tera
{# Creates blank lines #}
{% for item in items %}
{{ item }}
{% endfor %}

{# No extra whitespace #}
{%- for item in items %}
{{ item }}
{%- endfor %}
```

**Debugging workflow**:
1. Print template context: {{ __tera_context | json_encode() }}
2. Check CONSTRUCT output format (is it what template expects?)
3. Test template with minimal hardcoded data
4. Add data fields incrementally
5. Validate generated output with target language formatter
""" .

ps:TroubleshootingGuide_S4 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Generation Pipeline Issues" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 4 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Error**: "Generated code doesn't compile"
**Cause**: Template logic error or missing type mapping
**Solution**: Isolate the problematic generated file
```bash
# Run formatter to see syntax errors
rustfmt --check generated/domain_structs.rs

# Check specific compilation error
cargo build 2>&1 | grep "generated/"

# Fix in template or type mapping in CONSTRUCT
```

**Error**: "SHACL validation fails"
**Cause**: Ontology violates constraints
**Solution**: Read SHACL report, fix ontology
```
SHACL Violation: ps:MentalModelShift
  Property: ps:phase
  Expected: xsd:integer
  Found: "1" (string)

Fix: ps:phase 1 .  (not ps:phase "1" .)
```

**Error**: "Generation receipt hash mismatch"
**Cause**: Non-deterministic generation (template has random/time-dependent logic)
**Solution**: Remove non-deterministic elements
```tera
{# Non-deterministic #}
Generated at {{ now() }}

{# Deterministic - use receipt timestamp #}
Generated at {{ receipt.timestamp }}
```

**Error**: "ggen command not found"
**Cause**: ggen not installed or not in PATH
**Solution**: Install ggen
```bash
cargo install ggen
# or clone and build from source
git clone https://github.com/seanchatmangpt/ggen
cd ggen && cargo install --path .
```

**Error**: "Out of memory during generation"
**Cause**: Very large ontology or inefficient CONSTRUCT query
**Solution**: Optimize query or split into smaller rules
```sparql
# Inefficient - materializes huge cross product
WHERE { ?x a Type . ?y a Type . ?z a Type }

# Efficient - use specific joins
WHERE { ?x linkTo ?y . ?y linkTo ?z }
```

**Getting Help**:
- Check error message line number
- Search GitHub issues: https://github.com/seanchatmangpt/ggen/issues
- Ask in Discussions: https://github.com/seanchatmangpt/a2a-rs/discussions
- Include: ggen version, full error output, minimal reproduction ontology
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 3 - Advanced Content (P2)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:DeepRdfMentalModels a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "Deep RDF Mental Models: Advanced Concepts and Design Patterns" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/mental-models/DEEP-RDF.md" ;
    ps:phase 3 ;
    ps:priority "P2" ;
    ps:targetPersona "architect" ;
    ps:wordCount 2800 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "20 minutes" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S1, ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S2, ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S3, ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S4 .

ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Graph Thinking vs Tree Thinking" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """Most developers think in **trees** (class hierarchies, JSON documents, filesystem paths). RDF requires **graph thinking**.

**Tree mindset**:
- Single root
- One parent per node
- Hierarchical navigation (drill down)
- Ownership semantics (child belongs to parent)

**Graph mindset**:
- No root (enter from any node)
- Multiple types per node
- Relationship navigation (follow edges)
- Reference semantics (shared nodes)

**Example**: Representing a message in an agent system

**Tree approach** (JSON):
```json
{
  "message": {
    "id": "msg-123",
    "sender": {
      "agentId": "agent-1",
      "name": "Alice"
    },
    "content": "Hello",
    "task": {
      "taskId": "task-456",
      "status": "pending"
    }
  }
}
```

**Problem**: Sender and task are embedded. If they appear elsewhere, data is duplicated.

**Graph approach** (RDF):
```turtle
msg:msg-123 a a2a:Message ;
    a2a:messageId "msg-123" ;
    a2a:sentBy agent:agent-1 ;
    a2a:content "Hello" ;
    a2a:relatedTask task:task-456 .

agent:agent-1 a a2a:Agent ;
    a2a:agentId "agent-1" ;
    a2a:name "Alice" .

task:task-456 a a2a:Task ;
    a2a:taskId "task-456" ;
    a2a:status "pending" .
```

**Benefit**: Entities are independent. Query from any entry point. No duplication.

**Mental shift required**:
1. Entities are nodes, not nested objects
2. Properties are edges, not attributes
3. Navigation is bidirectional (can query "messages sent by agent" or "agent that sent message")
4. Sharing is natural (task can be related to multiple messages)

**Practice**: Take a JSON structure from your domain. Flatten it into RDF triples. Notice how relationships become explicit.
""" .

ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "RDF Reification and Blank Nodes" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Reification**: Making statements about statements.

**Use case**: Track metadata about triples (who asserted it, when, confidence score).

**Example**: "Alice says Bob's age is 30, asserted on 2026-02-09"

```turtle
# The base triple
ex:Bob ex:age 30 .

# Reified (statement about the statement)
[] a rdf:Statement ;
    rdf:subject ex:Bob ;
    rdf:predicate ex:age ;
    rdf:object 30 ;
    ex:assertedBy ex:Alice ;
    ex:assertedOn "2026-02-09"^^xsd:date .
```

**Blank nodes**: Anonymous nodes (no IRI).

**Syntax**: `[]` creates a blank node.

**Use case**: Temporary or structural nodes that don't need global identity.

**Example**: Address as blank node
```turtle
ex:Alice ex:address [
    ex:street "123 Main St" ;
    ex:city "Springfield" ;
    ex:zip "12345"
] .
```

**When to use blank nodes**:
- Structural nesting (like address above)
- Lists (rdf:List uses blank nodes internally)
- Reification
- **Avoid for**: Entities that might be referenced elsewhere (use IRIs instead)

**Named graphs**: Group triples into contexts.

**Example**: Track data provenance
```turtle
# Default graph
ex:Alice ex:age 30 .

# Named graph for source attribution
GRAPH ex:Source1 {
    ex:Alice ex:age 30 .
}

GRAPH ex:Source2 {
    ex:Alice ex:age 28 .
}
```

**Query**: Which sources claim Alice's age?
```sparql
SELECT ?graph ?age WHERE {
    GRAPH ?graph {
        ex:Alice ex:age ?age .
    }
}
```

**a2a-rs usage**: Named graphs could track which agent asserted which triples.
""" .

ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Ontology Design Patterns" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Pattern 1: Entity-Attribute-Value (EAV)**

Define entities with typed properties:
```turtle
a2a:AgentCard a a2a:Entity ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:agentId, a2a:name, a2a:capabilities .

a2a:agentId a2a:type xsd:string ;
    a2a:required true .
```

**When**: Domain types with known, fixed schema.

**Pattern 2: Enumeration**

Model closed sets of values:
```turtle
a2a:TaskStatus a a2a:EnumType ;
    a2a:hasVariant a2a:Pending, a2a:InProgress, a2a:Completed .

a2a:Pending a a2a:EnumVariant ;
    a2a:value "pending" .
```

**When**: Finite, named options (status codes, categories, etc.).

**Pattern 3: Polymorphic Types**

Entities with multiple types:
```turtle
ex:Alice a a2a:Agent, ex:Human, ex:Administrator .
```

**When**: Cross-cutting concerns (an agent can also be an admin, a human, etc.).

**Pattern 4: Property Restrictions**

SHACL constraints:
```turtle
a2a:AgentCardShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass a2a:AgentCard ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path a2a:agentId ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:datatype xsd:string ;
        sh:pattern "^agent-[a-z0-9]+$" ;
    ] .
```

**When**: Validation rules beyond simple types.

**Pattern 5: Versioning**

Track ontology evolution:
```turtle
a2a:AgentCardV1 a owl:Class ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:comment "Use a2a:AgentCard instead" .

a2a:AgentCard a owl:Class ;
    owl:versionInfo "2.0" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://ggen.io/ontology/a2a/v0.3.0> .
```

**When**: Ontology changes over time, need migration paths.

**Case study from a2a-rs**: See `ggen/ontology/a2a-message.ttl` for EAV pattern, `a2a-task.ttl` for enums.
""" .

ps:DeepRdfMentalModels_S4 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Inference and Reasoning" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 4 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**RDFS Inference**: Derive implicit triples from explicit ones.

**Example**: Class hierarchy
```turtle
a2a:Agent rdfs:subClassOf a2a:Entity .
ex:Alice a a2a:Agent .

# Inferred (via rdfs:subClassOf)
ex:Alice a a2a:Entity .
```

**Use case**: Query for all entities, get agents too (without explicitly typing them as both).

**OWL Inference**: More powerful reasoning.

**Example**: Transitive properties
```turtle
ex:locatedIn a owl:TransitiveProperty .

ex:Boston ex:locatedIn ex:Massachusetts .
ex:Massachusetts ex:locatedIn ex:USA .

# Inferred
ex:Boston ex:locatedIn ex:USA .
```

**Example**: Inverse properties
```turtle
a2a:sentBy owl:inverseOf a2a:sent .

msg:msg-123 a2a:sentBy agent:agent-1 .

# Inferred
agent:agent-1 a2a:sent msg:msg-123 .
```

**When to use inference**:
- Simplify queries (query for superclass, get subclasses)
- Derive relationships (inverse, transitive, symmetric)
- Consistency checking (detect logical contradictions)

**When NOT to use inference**:
- Performance-critical systems (reasoning is expensive)
- Ontologies change frequently (materialized views can become stale)
- Explicit is clearer (inference can surprise maintainers)

**a2a-rs approach**: Minimal inference. Most relationships are explicit. SHACL validates, doesn't infer.

**Recommendation**: Start with explicit triples. Add inference only when query simplification justifies complexity.

**Next**: See [Ontology Anti-Patterns](../anti-patterns/CATALOG.md) for what NOT to do.
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:OntologyAntiPatterns a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "Ontology Anti-Patterns: Common Mistakes and How to Fix Them" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/anti-patterns/CATALOG.md" ;
    ps:phase 3 ;
    ps:priority "P2" ;
    ps:targetPersona "architect" ;
    ps:wordCount 2600 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "18 minutes" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S1, ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S2, ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S3 .

ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Structural Anti-Patterns" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Anti-Pattern 1: God Class Ontology**

**Symptom**: One entity with 50+ properties.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:Agent a a2a:Entity ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:agentId, a2a:name, a2a:email, a2a:phone,
                    a2a:address, a2a:city, a2a:state, a2a:zip,
                    a2a:createdAt, a2a:updatedAt, a2a:lastSeen,
                    # ... 40 more properties
```

**Problem**: Unmaintainable, violates single responsibility, impossible to evolve.

**Fix**: Decompose into focused entities.
```turtle
a2a:Agent a a2a:Entity ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:agentId, a2a:name ;
    a2a:hasContactInfo a2a:ContactInfo ;
    a2a:hasMetadata a2a:AgentMetadata .

a2a:ContactInfo a a2a:Entity ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:email, a2a:phone, a2a:address .
```

**Anti-Pattern 2: Premature Abstraction**

**Symptom**: Deep class hierarchies with 1-2 subclasses each.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:Entity rdfs:subClassOf a2a:Thing .
a2a:Agent rdfs:subClassOf a2a:Entity .
a2a:HumanAgent rdfs:subClassOf a2a:Agent .
a2a:AIAgent rdfs:subClassOf a2a:Agent .
# Only 2 concrete types, 3 layers of abstraction
```

**Problem**: Overengineered, hard to navigate.

**Fix**: Flatten until you need the abstraction.
```turtle
# Just use types directly
a2a:HumanAgent a a2a:Entity .
a2a:AIAgent a a2a:Entity .

# Add superclass later if you actually query for "all agents"
```

**Anti-Pattern 3: Stringly-Typed Properties**

**Symptom**: Everything is xsd:string.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:createdAt a2a:type xsd:string .  # Should be xsd:dateTime
a2a:port a2a:type xsd:string .        # Should be xsd:integer
a2a:isActive a2a:type xsd:string .    # Should be xsd:boolean
```

**Problem**: No type safety, validation failures at runtime.

**Fix**: Use proper XSD types.
```turtle
a2a:createdAt a2a:type xsd:dateTime .
a2a:port a2a:type xsd:integer .
a2a:isActive a2a:type xsd:boolean .
```
""" .

ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Semantic Anti-Patterns" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Anti-Pattern 4: Namespace Pollution**

**Symptom**: Mixing concerns in one namespace.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:Agent ...
a2a:DatabaseConfig ...
a2a:HttpClient ...
a2a:LogLevel ...
# Domain + infrastructure + implementation in one namespace
```

**Problem**: Breaks separation of concerns, couples layers.

**Fix**: Use separate namespaces.
```turtle
a2a:Agent ...           # Domain
infra:DatabaseConfig ...  # Infrastructure
impl:HttpClient ...       # Implementation
```

**Anti-Pattern 5: Hidden Semantics in Names**

**Symptom**: Encoding type information in property names.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:agentIdString ...
a2a:portInteger ...
a2a:isActiveBoolean ...
```

**Problem**: Type is already in a2a:type, redundant in name.

**Fix**: Clean names, type in metadata.
```turtle
a2a:agentId a2a:type xsd:string .
a2a:port a2a:type xsd:integer .
a2a:isActive a2a:type xsd:boolean .
```

**Anti-Pattern 6: Implicit Constraints**

**Symptom**: Validation rules in comments, not SHACL.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:agentId a a2a:Property ;
    a2a:type xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "Must be non-empty and start with 'agent-'" .
```

**Problem**: Comments aren't validated, can drift from reality.

**Fix**: Explicit SHACL constraints.
```turtle
a2a:AgentShape sh:property [
    sh:path a2a:agentId ;
    sh:minLength 1 ;
    sh:pattern "^agent-.*" ;
] .
```
""" .

ps:OntologyAntiPatterns_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Evolution Anti-Patterns" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Anti-Pattern 7: Breaking Changes Without Versioning**

**Symptom**: Deleting or renaming properties with no migration path.

**Example**:
```turtle
# v1
a2a:agentName ...

# v2 (WRONG: just renamed)
a2a:name ...
```

**Problem**: All existing data breaks.

**Fix**: Deprecate old, introduce new, provide owl:equivalentProperty.
```turtle
# v2 (RIGHT)
a2a:agentName owl:deprecated true ;
    owl:equivalentProperty a2a:name .

a2a:name a a2a:Property .
```

**Anti-Pattern 8: No Change Documentation**

**Symptom**: Ontology changes with no changelog or version tags.

**Fix**: Use owl:versionInfo and maintain CHANGELOG.
```turtle
a2a:AgentCard owl:versionInfo "2.0" ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://ggen.io/ontology/a2a/v0.3.0> ;
    rdfs:comment "v2.0: Added capabilities property, deprecated agentName" .
```

**Anti-Pattern 9: Circular Dependencies**

**Symptom**: Entity A references B, B references A.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:Agent a2a:hasTask a2a:Task .
a2a:Task a2a:assignedTo a2a:Agent .
```

**Not always wrong**: Some domains have legitimate cycles. **But** watch for:
- Generation order issues (which to generate first?)
- Import cycles if split across files

**Fix options**:
1. Accept it (if domain requires bidirectional relationship)
2. Use owl:inverseOf (define one, infer the other)
3. Break cycle with intermediate entity

**Anti-Pattern 10: Copy-Paste Ontology Inheritance**

**Symptom**: Duplicating property definitions across similar entities.

**Example**:
```turtle
a2a:Agent a2a:hasProperty a2a:createdAt, a2a:updatedAt .
a2a:Task a2a:hasProperty a2a:createdAt, a2a:updatedAt .
a2a:Message a2a:hasProperty a2a:createdAt, a2a:updatedAt .
```

**Fix**: Use shared superclass or mixin pattern.
```turtle
a2a:Timestamped a a2a:Mixin ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:createdAt, a2a:updatedAt .

a2a:Agent a2a:includesMixin a2a:Timestamped .
a2a:Task a2a:includesMixin a2a:Timestamped .
```

**Refactoring Checklist**:
- [ ] No entity has 30+ properties
- [ ] Types are specific (not all xsd:string)
- [ ] SHACL constraints exist for validation rules
- [ ] Namespaces are separated by concern
- [ ] Breaking changes are versioned
- [ ] Common properties use mixins/superclasses

**Next**: Apply these to your ontology via [Migration Playbook](../migration/PLAYBOOK.md).
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:MigrationPlaybook a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "Migration Playbook: Transitioning to Ontology-Driven Development" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/migration/PLAYBOOK.md" ;
    ps:phase 3 ;
    ps:priority "P2" ;
    ps:targetPersona "architect" ;
    ps:wordCount 2800 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "20 minutes" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:MigrationPlaybook_S1, ps:MigrationPlaybook_S2, ps:MigrationPlaybook_S3, ps:MigrationPlaybook_S4, ps:MigrationPlaybook_S5 .

ps:MigrationPlaybook_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Migration Strategy Overview" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**DO NOT attempt big-bang migration.** Incremental adoption is safer and proves value early.

**Three-Phase Approach**:

**Phase 1: Pilot (2-4 weeks)**
- Select 5-10 core domain types
- Design ontology
- Generate code
- Integrate alongside existing hand-written code
- **Goal**: Prove feasibility, identify issues, build team confidence

**Phase 2: Expand (2-3 months)**
- Migrate 50-80% of domain layer
- Establish patterns and templates
- Train team
- **Goal**: Reach critical mass where ontology is primary source of truth

**Phase 3: Consolidate (3-6 months)**
- Migrate remaining edge cases
- Refine templates
- Document patterns
- **Goal**: 80%+ domain layer generated, maintainable ontology

**Success Criteria**:
- Generated code compiles and passes tests
- Team can extend ontology independently
- Feature velocity equal or better than hand-written
- Zero regression bugs from migration

**Risk Mitigation**:
- Keep hand-written code working in parallel during pilot
- Feature flag generated vs manual code paths
- Gradual rollout (dev → staging → production)
- Rollback plan if pilot fails
""" .

ps:MigrationPlaybook_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Step 1: Assessment and Selection" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Assess Current Codebase**:

1. **Inventory domain types**:
   - Count structs/classes/interfaces in domain layer
   - Identify relationships and dependencies
   - Classify by complexity (simple data types vs complex behavior)

2. **Identify candidates for migration**:
   - ✅ **Good candidates**: Data structures, API contracts, serialization types
   - ⚠️ **Marginal**: Stateful entities with complex behavior
   - ❌ **Poor candidates**: Algorithms, business logic, UI components

3. **Select pilot scope**:
   - Pick 5-10 types that are:
     - Central to domain (used widely)
     - Simple (few dependencies)
     - Multi-platform (benefit from consistency)
   - Example for a2a-rs: AgentCard, Message, Task (core protocol types)

**Assessment Template**:

| Type | Complexity | Multi-Platform? | Candidate? | Priority |
|------|------------|-----------------|------------|----------|
| AgentCard | Low | Yes (Rust + TS) | ✅ Yes | High |
| Message | Low | Yes | ✅ Yes | High |
| TaskManager | High (stateful) | No | ❌ No | N/A |
| ValidationRule | Medium | Yes | ⚠️ Maybe | Medium |

**Deliverable**: List of 5-10 types for pilot migration.
""" .

ps:MigrationPlaybook_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Step 2: Ontology Design" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Design Ontology for Pilot Types**:

1. **Create ontology file** (e.g., `ontology/domain.ttl`):
```turtle
@prefix dm: <https://example.org/ontology/domain/> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

dm:AgentCard a dm:Entity ;
    rdfs:comment "Represents an agent's identity and capabilities" ;
    dm:hasProperty dm:agentId, dm:name, dm:capabilities .

dm:agentId a dm:Property ;
    dm:type xsd:string ;
    dm:required true ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique agent identifier (URI format)" .
```

2. **Map existing code to RDF**:
   - For each struct field → dm:Property
   - For each type → dm:type (xsd:string, xsd:integer, etc.)
   - For each validation rule → SHACL constraint

3. **Add constraints** (SHACL):
```turtle
dm:AgentCardShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass dm:AgentCard ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path dm:agentId ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:pattern "^https?://" ;
    ] .
```

4. **Validate ontology**:
```bash
rapper -i turtle ontology/domain.ttl  # Syntax check
# SHACL validation (if tool available)
```

**Common Pitfalls**:
- Over-modeling (don't capture every implementation detail)
- Under-constraining (add SHACL rules for invariants)
- Ignoring existing patterns (study a2a-rs ontology first)

**Deliverable**: Valid Turtle ontology file for pilot types.
""" .

ps:MigrationPlaybook_S4 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Step 3: CONSTRUCT Queries and Templates" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 4 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Write CONSTRUCT Queries**:

Create `ggen.toml`:
```toml
[project]
name = "domain-codegen"

[ontology]
source = "ontology/domain.ttl"

[[rules]]
name = "domain-structs"
template = "templates/structs.tera"
output = "generated/domain.rs"
construct = """
PREFIX dm: <https://example.org/ontology/domain/>

CONSTRUCT {
    ?entity a dm:GeneratedStruct ;
        dm:structName ?name ;
        dm:hasField ?prop .
    ?prop a dm:StructField ;
        dm:fieldName ?propName ;
        dm:fieldType ?rustType .
}
WHERE {
    ?entity a dm:Entity ; dm:name ?name .
    ?entity dm:hasProperty ?prop .
    ?prop dm:name ?propName ; dm:type ?type .
    # Map types: xsd:string → String, etc.
}
"""
```

**Create Tera Templates**:

`templates/structs.tera`:
```tera
{%- for struct in structs %}
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct {{ struct.name }} {
    {%- for field in struct.fields %}
    pub {{ field.name }}: {{ field.rust_type }},
    {%- endfor %}
}
{%- endfor %}
```

**Test Generation**:
```bash
ggen generate --config ggen.toml
rustfmt generated/domain.rs
cargo check  # Verify it compiles
```

**Iterate**:
- Fix type mappings in CONSTRUCT
- Adjust template formatting
- Add missing derives, attributes

**Deliverable**: Working ggen pipeline that generates compiling code.
""" .

ps:MigrationPlaybook_S5 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Step 4: Integration and Rollout" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 5 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Integration Steps**:

1. **Parallel implementation**:
```rust
// Old hand-written code (keep for now)
mod domain_manual;

// New generated code
mod generated;

// Adapter to bridge
pub use generated::AgentCard;  // Prefer generated
// pub use domain_manual::AgentCard;  // Fallback
```

2. **Feature flag**:
```rust
#[cfg(feature = "generated-domain")]
pub use generated::*;

#[cfg(not(feature = "generated-domain"))]
pub use domain_manual::*;
```

3. **Test both paths**:
```rust
#[test]
fn test_agent_card_roundtrip() {
    let card = AgentCard { ... };
    let json = serde_json::to_string(&card).unwrap();
    let deserialized: AgentCard = serde_json::from_str(&json).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(card, deserialized);
}
```

4. **Gradual rollout**:
   - Week 1: Enable in dev environment
   - Week 2: Enable in staging, monitor
   - Week 3: Enable in production for 10% traffic (canary)
   - Week 4: Full rollout if no issues

**Rollback Plan**:
- Keep hand-written code for 1-2 release cycles
- If issues arise, disable feature flag
- Fix ontology/templates, regenerate
- Re-test before re-enabling

**Post-Migration Cleanup**:
- Once stable (4+ weeks), delete hand-written code
- Update imports to use generated types directly
- Document ontology as new source of truth
- Train team on extending ontology

**Monitoring**:
- Track: Compilation errors, test failures, runtime errors
- Compare: Performance, memory usage (generated vs manual)
- Validate: Serialization compatibility (JSON output identical)

**Success Metrics**:
- [ ] Generated code compiles with zero warnings
- [ ] All tests pass
- [ ] No performance regression (< 5% slower)
- [ ] Zero production incidents related to generated code
- [ ] Team can extend ontology without intervention

**Next**: Expand to Phase 2 (50-80% of domain layer).
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:Exercise03 a ps:Exercise ;
    ps:title "Exercise 03: Template-Driven Code Generation" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/exercises/03-template-generation/README.md" ;
    ps:exerciseNumber 3 ;
    ps:difficulty "intermediate" ;
    ps:phase 3 ;
    ps:priority "P2" ;
    ps:targetPersona "developer" ;
    ps:estimatedTime "50 minutes" ;
    ps:prerequisite ps:Exercise02 ;
    ps:learningObjective "Create a Tera template that generates Rust code from CONSTRUCT query results" ;
    ps:successCriteria "Template generates valid Rust code", "Generated code compiles with rustfmt", "You understand how CONSTRUCT output feeds templates" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:Exercise03_S1, ps:Exercise03_S2, ps:Exercise03_S3 .

ps:Exercise03_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Goal" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """By the end of this exercise, you will:
- Understand how CONSTRUCT query results become template context
- Write a Tera template that emits Rust structs
- Handle arrays, optional fields, and type mappings
- Validate generated code compiles

**Time**: 45-60 minutes
**Prerequisites**: Exercise 02 (SPARQL CONSTRUCT)
""" .

ps:Exercise03_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Instructions" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Part 1: Understand CONSTRUCT Output Format**

Given this CONSTRUCT query from Exercise 02:
```sparql
CONSTRUCT {
    ?entity a ex:GeneratedStruct ;
        ex:name ?name ;
        ex:hasField ?field .
    ?field ex:fieldName ?fieldName ;
        ex:fieldType ?fieldType .
}
WHERE { ... }
```

The output is an RDF graph. Templates receive this as JSON context:
```json
{
  "structs": [
    {
      "name": "Person",
      "fields": [
        {"fieldName": "name", "fieldType": "String"},
        {"fieldName": "age", "fieldType": "i32"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Part 2: Write Tera Template**

Create `templates/rust_structs.tera`:
```tera
{#- TODO: Iterate over structs array -#}
{%- for struct in structs %}

{#- TODO: Generate struct definition -#}
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct {{ struct.name }} {
    {#- TODO: Iterate over fields -#}
    {%- for field in struct.fields %}
    pub {{ field.fieldName }}: {{ field.fieldType }},
    {%- endfor %}
}

{%- endfor %}
```

**Part 3: Test Generation**

1. Run template against test data (use ggen or Tera playground)
2. Expected output:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Person {
    pub name: String,
    pub age: i32,
}
```

3. Validate with rustfmt:
```bash
rustfmt --check output.rs
```

**Part 4: Add Complexity**

Extend template to handle:
- Optional fields: `Option<T>`
- Serde annotations: `#[serde(rename = "...")]`
- Doc comments: `/// ...`

**Bonus**: Generate both struct definition AND impl block with a constructor.
""" .

ps:Exercise03_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Success Criteria and Common Mistakes" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Success Criteria**:
- [ ] Template iterates over structs and fields correctly
- [ ] Generated code compiles (`cargo check`)
- [ ] Generated code formats cleanly (`rustfmt --check`)
- [ ] Optional fields wrapped in `Option<T>`
- [ ] You can explain: "Templates fold over CONSTRUCT output; they don't rebuild structure"

**Common Mistakes**:
- Forgetting `{%-` for whitespace control → extra blank lines
- Not escaping reserved keywords (e.g., `type` is Rust keyword)
- Assuming fields are always present (use `| default` filter)
- Rebuilding structure in template (belongs in CONSTRUCT!)

**Solution**:
Available in `exercises/03-template-generation/solution/` after attempting the exercise.

**Next**: Move on to [Exercise 04: Ontology Design Patterns] (intermediate level).
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 4 - Capstone and Advanced Materials (P3)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:CaseStudy02 a ps:CaseStudy ;
    ps:title "Case Study: Multi-Language Protocol Consistency" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/case-studies/02-MULTI-LANGUAGE.md" ;
    ps:phase 4 ;
    ps:priority "P3" ;
    ps:targetPersona "architect" ;
    ps:estimatedTime "15 minutes" ;
    ps:wordCount 2400 ;
    ps:challenge "Maintaining A2A Protocol consistency across Rust, Python, and TypeScript implementations. Manual synchronization led to type mismatches, validation drift, and integration bugs between platforms." ;
    ps:solution "Single RDF ontology defining A2A protocol semantics. Language-specific CONSTRUCT queries extract entity structure. Tera templates generate Rust structs, Python Pydantic models, TypeScript Zod schemas. CI validates cross-language serialization compatibility." ;
    ps:result "Three implementations guaranteed consistent via shared ontology. Protocol updates propagate to all languages in single commit. Zero cross-language bugs in 6 months. Feature velocity increased 3× for multi-platform changes." ;
    ps:hasSection ps:CaseStudy02_S1, ps:CaseStudy02_S2, ps:CaseStudy02_S3, ps:CaseStudy02_S4 .

ps:CaseStudy02_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "The Challenge: Protocol Drift Across Languages" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Context**: The A2A Protocol needs implementations in:
- **Rust** (a2a-rs): High-performance agent runtime
- **Python** (a2a-py): ML integration and scripting
- **TypeScript** (a2a-ts): Web UI and browser agents

**The Manual Approach** (what we tried first):
1. Define protocol in spec/MESSAGE.md (prose)
2. Implement in Rust: `pub struct Message { ... }`
3. Implement in Python: `class Message(BaseModel): ...`
4. Implement in TypeScript: `interface Message { ... }`

**Problems Encountered**:

| Issue | Example | Impact |
|-------|---------|--------|
| Type mismatches | Rust uses `chrono::DateTime`, Python uses `datetime`, TS uses `Date` | Serialization bugs |
| Validation drift | Rust validates `message_id` regex, Python doesn't | Invalid data accepted |
| Field naming | Rust uses `snake_case`, TS uses `camelCase`, manual mapping needed | Serialization errors |
| Missing fields | New field added to Rust, forgotten in Python | Runtime errors |
| Documentation | Comments in Rust code, not propagated to Python/TS | Developer confusion |

**Real incident** (January 2025):
- Rust added `priority: Option<u8>` field to Task
- TypeScript implementation missed it
- Web UI sent tasks without priority
- Backend rejected them (validation failure)
- 2 hours debugging to find the mismatch
- **Root cause**: No single source of truth
""" .

ps:CaseStudy02_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "The Solution: Ontology as Universal Specification" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Architecture**:

```
spec/MESSAGE.md (human-readable spec)
        ↓
ggen/ontology/a2a-message.ttl (RDF representation)
        ↓
   ┌────┴────┬─────────┬─────────┐
   ↓         ↓         ↓         ↓
CONSTRUCT CONSTRUCT CONSTRUCT CONSTRUCT
(Rust)    (Python)  (TS)      (JSON Schema)
   ↓         ↓         ↓         ↓
Tera      Tera      Tera      Tera
template  template  template  template
   ↓         ↓         ↓         ↓
domain/   models/   types/    schemas/
message.rs message.py message.ts message.json
```

**Step 1: Define Ontology**

`ggen/ontology/a2a-message.ttl`:
```turtle
a2a:Message a a2a:Entity ;
    rdfs:comment "Represents an A2A protocol message" ;
    a2a:hasProperty a2a:messageId, a2a:content, a2a:sender, a2a:timestamp .

a2a:messageId a a2a:Property ;
    a2a:type xsd:string ;
    a2a:required true ;
    a2a:pattern "^msg-[a-f0-9]{8}-[a-f0-9]{4}-[a-f0-9]{4}-[a-f0-9]{4}-[a-f0-9]{12}$" ;
    a2a:jsonName "messageId" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unique message identifier in UUID format" .

a2a:timestamp a a2a:Property ;
    a2a:type xsd:dateTime ;
    a2a:required true ;
    rdfs:comment "Message creation timestamp in ISO 8601 format" .
```

**Step 2: Language-Specific CONSTRUCT Queries**

`ggen/rust.toml`:
```sparql
CONSTRUCT {
    ?entity a a2a:RustStruct ;
        a2a:structName ?name ;
        a2a:hasField ?prop .
    ?prop a2a:rustFieldName ?snakeName ;
        a2a:rustType ?rustType ;
        a2a:serdeRename ?jsonName .
}
WHERE {
    ?entity a a2a:Entity ; a2a:name ?name .
    ?entity a2a:hasProperty ?prop .
    ?prop a2a:name ?propName ; a2a:type ?type ; a2a:jsonName ?jsonName .

    # Convert to snake_case for Rust
    # Map xsd:dateTime → chrono::DateTime<Utc>
}
```

`ggen/python.toml`:
```sparql
CONSTRUCT {
    ?entity a a2a:PydanticModel ;
        a2a:className ?name ;
        a2a:hasField ?prop .
    ?prop a2a:pythonFieldName ?snakeName ;
        a2a:pythonType ?pyType ;
        a2a:fieldValidator ?validator .
}
WHERE {
    # Map xsd:dateTime → datetime
    # Extract SHACL constraints → Pydantic validators
}
```

**Step 3: Templates Generate Language-Specific Code**

Templates emit idiomatic code for each language, all from the same ontology source.
""" .

ps:CaseStudy02_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Results and Metrics" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Quantitative Results**:

| Metric | Before Ontology | After Ontology | Improvement |
|--------|----------------|----------------|-------------|
| Cross-language bugs per month | 8-12 | 0 | 100% reduction |
| Protocol update time (all langs) | 3-5 days | 2 hours | 95% reduction |
| Test coverage (serialization) | 60% | 100% | N/A |
| Time to add new message type | 6 hours | 1.5 hours | 75% reduction |
| Lines of hand-written domain code | ~3000 | ~600 | 80% reduction |

**Qualitative Benefits**:

1. **Guaranteed Consistency**: Structural impossibility for drift (all code from one source)
2. **Spec Compliance**: JSON schema generated from ontology → round-trip validation ensures spec adherence
3. **Developer Confidence**: "If it compiles in one language, it's correct in all languages"
4. **Faster Onboarding**: New contributors learn ontology, get consistency in all languages
5. **Easier Maintenance**: Change ontology once, regenerate everywhere

**CI Pipeline Integration**:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ontology-validation.yml
- name: Validate ontology
  run: rapper -i turtle ggen/ontology/*.ttl

- name: Generate code (all languages)
  run: ggen generate --all

- name: Test Rust
  run: cargo test

- name: Test Python
  run: pytest

- name: Test TypeScript
  run: npm test

- name: Cross-language serialization test
  run: |
    # Rust generates JSON → Python deserializes → TS deserializes
    # All must succeed and produce identical objects
```

**Unexpected Benefit**: TypeScript gained runtime validation (Zod schemas) for free, which it didn't have before. Previously only had compile-time types.
""" .

ps:CaseStudy02_S4 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Lessons Learned and Replicability" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 4 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**What Worked Well**:

1. **Type mapping strategy**: Explicit mapping tables (xsd:dateTime → language-specific types) in CONSTRUCT queries
2. **Validation extraction**: SHACL constraints → Pydantic validators, Zod rules, Rust builder checks
3. **Naming conventions**: Ontology uses consistent naming, CONSTRUCT queries handle language conventions (snake_case vs camelCase)
4. **Incremental adoption**: Started with Message type, proved concept, expanded to all 15 protocol types

**Challenges**:

1. **Template complexity**: Some language idioms are hard to express generically
   - **Solution**: Language-specific templates, shared patterns via Tera macros
2. **Type mapping edge cases**: Not all XSD types have direct language equivalents
   - **Solution**: Custom type annotations in ontology (e.g., `a2a:rustType "Url"`)
3. **Tooling learning curve**: Team needed to learn RDF, SPARQL, Tera
   - **Solution**: 4-week training program, pair programming during migration

**Replicability Checklist**:

- [ ] Multi-language project (2+ target languages)
- [ ] Shared protocol or API contract
- [ ] High change frequency (weekly+ updates)
- [ ] Team has 1-2 members who can learn RDF/SPARQL (doesn't need to be everyone)
- [ ] CI pipeline can run code generation step

**If all checkboxes true**: Strong candidate for ontology-driven approach.

**Assets Available**:

- Full ontology: `ggen/ontology/` in a2a-rs repo
- CONSTRUCT queries: `ggen/*.toml`
- Templates: `ggen/templates/`
- CI config: `.github/workflows/`

**Next Steps** (for your project):

1. Identify shared domain model across languages
2. Design ontology for 3-5 core types (pilot)
3. Write CONSTRUCT queries for each target language
4. Create templates for each language
5. Generate, test, validate
6. Expand to full domain model

**Timeline**: 4-8 weeks for pilot (including learning curve), 2-4 months for full migration.
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:Exercise07 a ps:Exercise ;
    ps:title "Exercise 07: Full-Stack Generation" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/exercises/07-full-stack/README.md" ;
    ps:exerciseNumber 7 ;
    ps:difficulty "advanced" ;
    ps:phase 4 ;
    ps:priority "P3" ;
    ps:targetPersona "developer" ;
    ps:estimatedTime "2.5 hours" ;
    ps:prerequisite ps:Exercise03 ;
    ps:learningObjective "Generate complete domain type + port trait + basic adapter from ontology, end-to-end" ;
    ps:successCriteria "Generated code compiles", "Tests pass", "Adapter integrates with a2a-rs", "You understand full hexagonal architecture generation" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:Exercise07_S1, ps:Exercise07_S2, ps:Exercise07_S3 .

ps:Exercise07_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Capstone Goal" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """This is a capstone exercise combining all skills:
- Ontology design (μ₁)
- SPARQL CONSTRUCT queries (μ₂)
- Tera templates (μ₃)
- Integration with a2a-rs

**Scenario**: Add a new feature to a2a-rs: **Agent Capability Registry**

Agents can declare capabilities (e.g., "text-generation", "image-processing"). The system needs:
1. **Domain type**: `Capability` struct
2. **Port trait**: `CapabilityRegistry` interface
3. **Adapter**: In-memory implementation

**Time**: 2-3 hours (plan for breaks)
**Prerequisites**: Exercises 01-06, familiarity with a2a-rs codebase
""" .

ps:Exercise07_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Step-by-Step Instructions" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Part 1: Design Ontology (30 min)**

Create `ggen/ontology/capability.ttl`:

```turtle
@prefix a2a: <https://ggen.io/ontology/a2a/> .
@prefix cap: <https://ggen.io/ontology/capability/> .

# Domain Entity
cap:Capability a a2a:Entity ;
    rdfs:comment "Represents an agent capability" ;
    a2a:hasProperty cap:capabilityId, cap:name, cap:description, cap:version .

cap:capabilityId a a2a:Property ;
    a2a:type xsd:string ;
    a2a:required true ;
    a2a:pattern "^cap-[a-z0-9-]+$" .

# Port Trait
cap:CapabilityRegistry a a2a:Port ;
    rdfs:comment "Interface for managing agent capabilities" ;
    a2a:hasMethod cap:register, cap:lookup, cap:list .

cap:register a a2a:PortMethod ;
    a2a:name "register_capability" ;
    a2a:async true ;
    a2a:hasParam [ a2a:name "capability" ; a2a:type "Capability" ] ;
    a2a:returnType "Result<(), RegistryError>" .

# Continue for lookup and list methods...
```

**Part 2: CONSTRUCT Query (30 min)**

Add to `ggen/ggen.toml`:

```toml
[[rules]]
name = "capability-domain"
template = "templates/rust-domain-structs.tera"
output = "generated/capability.rs"
construct = """
PREFIX cap: <https://ggen.io/ontology/capability/>
CONSTRUCT {
    # Shape entity for domain struct template
    # ... (fill in based on Exercise 03 patterns)
}
WHERE {
    ?entity a a2a:Entity .
    # ... (pattern match Capability)
}
"""

[[rules]]
name = "capability-port"
template = "templates/rust-port-traits.tera"
output = "generated/capability_port.rs"
construct = """
# Shape port for trait template
# ... (based on a2a-rs existing port rules)
"""
```

**Part 3: Generate and Validate (20 min)**

```bash
cd ggen
ggen generate --config ggen.toml
rustfmt ../a2a-rs/src/generated/capability*.rs
cd ../a2a-rs
cargo build --all-features
```

Fix any errors by adjusting ontology or templates.

**Part 4: Implement Adapter (30 min)**

Hand-write adapter (adapters are NOT generated):

`a2a-rs/src/adapter/capability/in_memory.rs`:
```rust
use crate::generated::capability::{Capability, CapabilityRegistry};
use std::collections::HashMap;

pub struct InMemoryCapabilityRegistry {
    capabilities: HashMap<String, Capability>,
}

#[async_trait]
impl CapabilityRegistry for InMemoryCapabilityRegistry {
    async fn register_capability(&mut self, cap: Capability) -> Result<(), RegistryError> {
        self.capabilities.insert(cap.capability_id.clone(), cap);
        Ok(())
    }
    // ... implement lookup and list
}
```

**Part 5: Integration Test (30 min)**

Write test:
```rust
#[tokio::test]
async fn test_capability_registry() {
    let mut registry = InMemoryCapabilityRegistry::new();
    let cap = Capability {
        capability_id: "cap-text-gen".to_string(),
        name: "Text Generation".to_string(),
        // ...
    };
    registry.register_capability(cap.clone()).await.unwrap();
    let result = registry.lookup("cap-text-gen").await.unwrap();
    assert_eq!(result.capability_id, cap.capability_id);
}
```
""" .

ps:Exercise07_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Success Criteria and Reflection" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Success Criteria**:
- [ ] Ontology validates (rapper, SHACL)
- [ ] CONSTRUCT queries produce expected IR
- [ ] Templates generate valid Rust code
- [ ] Generated code compiles with no warnings
- [ ] Adapter implements port trait correctly
- [ ] Tests pass
- [ ] You can explain the full pipeline: ontology → CONSTRUCT → template → code → adapter

**Reflection Questions**:

1. What percentage of this feature was generated vs hand-written?
   - Expected: ~70% generated (domain + port), ~30% hand-written (adapter + tests)

2. If you needed to add a new method to `CapabilityRegistry`, what would you change?
   - Add method to `cap:CapabilityRegistry` in ontology
   - Regenerate port trait
   - Implement method in adapter
   - No changes to domain types

3. How would you add a Python client for this registry?
   - Same ontology
   - New CONSTRUCT query for Python
   - Tera template for Pydantic model + async client
   - Guaranteed compatibility with Rust implementation

**Bonus Challenges**:
- Add SHACL validation for capability IDs
- Generate property-based tests using proptest
- Create OpenAPI spec from ontology
- Design migration from v1 to v2 (add new field)

**Graduation**: You've completed the advanced track. You're now ready to apply ontology-driven development to real projects.

**Next Steps**:
- Contribute to a2a-rs ontology
- Join [30-Day Challenge](../../community/30-DAY-CHALLENGE.md) for continuous practice
- Apply to your own domain model
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:ThirtyDayChallenge a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "30-Day Ontology-Driven Development Challenge" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/community/30-DAY-CHALLENGE.md" ;
    ps:phase 4 ;
    ps:priority "P3" ;
    ps:targetPersona "developer" ;
    ps:wordCount 2200 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "5 minutes to read, 30 days to complete" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S1, ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S2, ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S3, ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S4 .

ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Challenge Overview" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Goal**: Master ontology-driven development through daily practice.

**Format**:
- 30 daily challenges (weekdays only, ~6 weeks calendar time)
- 30-60 minutes per day
- Cumulative (each day builds on previous)
- Community-driven (share progress, get feedback)

**Outcome**: By day 30, you will:
- Design clean ontologies for complex domains
- Write efficient CONSTRUCT queries
- Create production-ready templates
- Integrate generated code with real projects
- Teach others the paradigm shift

**Time Commitment**: 30 hours total (~1 hour/day × 30 days)

**Prerequisites**: Basic programming experience (any language)

**Community Features**:
- Daily check-in thread (GitHub Discussions)
- Peer review (share your ontology, get feedback)
- Office hours (weekly Q&A with maintainers)
- Leaderboard (optional, based on completion %)
- Certificate upon completion (digital badge)
""" .

ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Week 1-2: Foundations (Days 1-10)" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Day 1**: Install tools (rapper, SPARQL engine, ggen)
**Day 2**: Write 10 RDF triples about your favorite domain
**Day 3**: Validate ontology with rapper, fix syntax errors
**Day 4**: Write first CONSTRUCT query (transform your triples)
**Day 5**: Tera template basics (emit simple text from JSON)

**Day 6**: Study a2a-rs ontology structure (ggen/ontology/a2a-agent.ttl)
**Day 7**: Design ontology for a simple domain (5 entities, 15 properties)
**Day 8**: Write CONSTRUCT query to extract entity-property structure
**Day 9**: Create Tera template to emit markdown table from CONSTRUCT
**Day 10**: Generate documentation from your ontology

**Weekend Reflection**: What's the hardest concept so far? Share in community.

**Week 1-2 Success**: You can design a simple ontology, query it, and emit output.
""" .

ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Week 3-4: Intermediate (Days 11-20)" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Day 11**: Add SHACL constraints to your ontology
**Day 12**: Violate a SHACL rule, observe validation failure
**Day 13**: Write CONSTRUCT query to generate Rust struct definitions
**Day 14**: Create Tera template for Rust code generation
**Day 15**: Generate Rust code, compile with rustfmt

**Day 16**: Study anti-patterns catalog, audit your ontology
**Day 17**: Refactor your ontology to fix 2 anti-patterns
**Day 18**: Design ontology for a multi-platform scenario (Rust + Python)
**Day 19**: Write language-specific CONSTRUCT queries (type mappings)
**Day 20**: Generate both Rust and Python from same ontology

**Weekend Reflection**: How does ontology-driven development change your design thinking?

**Week 3-4 Success**: You can generate production-quality code for multiple languages.
""" .

ps:ThirtyDayChallenge_S4 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Week 5-6: Advanced (Days 21-30)" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 4 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Day 21**: Choose a real feature from your work project
**Day 22**: Design ontology for that feature (10+ entities)
**Day 23**: Write CONSTRUCT queries for domain types
**Day 24**: Write CONSTRUCT queries for port traits
**Day 25**: Generate domain layer code

**Day 26**: Hand-write adapter implementing generated port
**Day 27**: Write property-based tests (proptest) for generated types
**Day 28**: Integrate with existing codebase (feature flag approach)
**Day 29**: Measure: Generated vs hand-written ratio, compilation time, test coverage
**Day 30**: Document your approach, share case study with community

**Final Reflection**:
- Would you use this on your next project?
- What ROI do you expect?
- What would you teach differently to a colleague?

**Completion Certificate**:

Upon finishing all 30 days:
1. Submit completion form with links to your ontology repo
2. Receive digital badge: "Ontology-Driven Development Practitioner"
3. Optional: Present your day 21-30 project in community showcase

**Next Steps After Challenge**:
- Contribute to a2a-rs ontology
- Mentor new challenge participants
- Apply to production systems
- Write blog post sharing your experience

**Join the Challenge**:
- Start date: Rolling (start anytime)
- Community: https://github.com/seanchatmangpt/a2a-rs/discussions
- Hashtag: #30DaysOntologyDriven
""" .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ps:SuccessMetrics a ps:FundamentalDoc ;
    ps:title "Success Metrics Dashboard: Measuring Ontology-Driven Development Impact" ;
    ps:filePath "paradigm-shift/metrics/DASHBOARD.md" ;
    ps:phase 4 ;
    ps:priority "P3" ;
    ps:targetPersona "manager" ;
    ps:wordCount 1800 ;
    ps:estimatedTime "12 minutes" ;
    ps:hasSection ps:SuccessMetrics_S1, ps:SuccessMetrics_S2, ps:SuccessMetrics_S3 .

ps:SuccessMetrics_S1 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Key Metrics to Track" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 1 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Adoption Metrics**:

| Metric | Definition | Target | How to Measure |
|--------|------------|--------|----------------|
| Ontology Coverage | % of domain types defined in ontology | 80%+ | Count entities in ontology / total domain types |
| Generation Ratio | % of domain code generated vs hand-written | 70%+ | LOC generated / total domain LOC |
| Developer Adoption | % of team extending ontology monthly | 60%+ | Git commits to ontology/ total devs |
| Time to First Generation | Days from onboarding to first generated code | <14 days | Survey new team members |

**Quality Metrics**:

| Metric | Definition | Target | How to Measure |
|--------|------------|--------|----------------|
| Cross-Platform Drift Bugs | Bugs caused by inconsistency between platforms | 0 | Tag bugs in issue tracker |
| Spec Compliance Rate | % of generated code passing spec validation | 100% | JSON schema validation in CI |
| Generated Code Warnings | Compiler warnings in generated code | 0 | Parse CI output |
| SHACL Validation Pass Rate | % of ontology commits passing SHACL validation | 100% | Track CI failures |

**Velocity Metrics**:

| Metric | Definition | Target | How to Measure |
|--------|------------|--------|----------------|
| Feature Implementation Time | Hours to implement multi-platform feature | -50% vs baseline | Track time in issue tracking |
| Protocol Update Propagation | Hours from spec change to all platforms updated | <4 hours | Timestamp commit → deploy |
| Onboarding Time | Days for new dev to be productive | <30 days | Survey new hires |
| Bug Fix Time (domain) | Average hours to fix domain-layer bug | <2 hours | MTTR from issue tracker |
""" .

ps:SuccessMetrics_S2 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Baseline and Target Setting" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 2 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Establishing Baseline** (before ontology adoption):

**Week 1-2**: Measure current state
```
# Adoption (baseline = 0% since not using ontology yet)
Domain types: [count manual structs across all platforms]
Code duplication: [count repeated definitions]

# Quality
Cross-platform bugs (last 6 months): [count from issue tracker]
Spec violations (last quarter): [count from bug reports]

# Velocity
Avg feature time (multi-platform): [survey last 10 features]
Protocol update time: [measure last 3 updates]
```

**Setting Targets**:

**Month 3** (after pilot):
- Ontology coverage: 30% (pilot types)
- Generation ratio: 50%
- Cross-platform bugs: -50% vs baseline

**Month 6** (after expansion):
- Ontology coverage: 70%
- Generation ratio: 70%
- Cross-platform bugs: -90% vs baseline
- Feature time: -40% for multi-platform changes

**Month 12** (mature):
- Ontology coverage: 80%+
- Generation ratio: 80%+
- Cross-platform bugs: 0
- Feature time: -60% for multi-platform changes

**Tracking Frequency**:
- Daily: CI metrics (SHACL pass rate, compiler warnings)
- Weekly: Bug counts, commit activity
- Monthly: Feature velocity, coverage ratios
- Quarterly: Team surveys, ROI calculation
""" .

ps:SuccessMetrics_S3 a ps:Section ;
    ps:sectionTitle "Dashboard and Reporting" ;
    ps:sectionLevel 2 ;
    ps:sectionOrder 3 ;
    ps:sectionContent """**Automated Metrics Collection**:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/metrics.yml
- name: Calculate ontology coverage
  run: |
    ONTOLOGY_ENTITIES=$(grep -c "a a2a:Entity" ggen/ontology/*.ttl)
    MANUAL_TYPES=$(grep -rc "pub struct" src/domain | awk '{sum+=$1} END {print sum}')
    COVERAGE=$(echo "scale=2; $ONTOLOGY_ENTITIES / ($ONTOLOGY_ENTITIES + $MANUAL_TYPES) * 100" | bc)
    echo "ontology_coverage=$COVERAGE%" >> $GITHUB_ENV

- name: Report to dashboard
  run: |
    curl -X POST https://metrics.example.com/api/report \
      -d "coverage=${{ env.ontology_coverage }}" \
      -d "date=$(date -I)"
```

**Dashboard Visualization** (example):

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Ontology-Driven Development Dashboard                  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                         │
│  Ontology Coverage:  [████████░░] 82%  ↑ +5% MoM      │
│  Generation Ratio:   [████████░░] 78%  ↑ +3% MoM      │
│                                                         │
│  Cross-Platform Bugs:     0  ↓ -100% vs baseline       │
│  Spec Compliance:       100%  → (maintained)            │
│                                                         │
│  Feature Velocity:      -58%  ↓ (faster)               │
│  Onboarding Time:    24 days  ↓ -20% QoQ              │
│                                                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Recent Activity:                                        │
│  • 3 entities added to ontology (this week)            │
│  • 5 CONSTRUCT queries updated                         │
│  • 0 SHACL validation failures (this month)            │
│  • 2 new team members completed training               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Monthly Report Template**:

```markdown
## Ontology-Driven Development: Month X Report

### Highlights
- 🎯 Achieved 80% ontology coverage milestone
- 🐛 Zero cross-platform bugs for 3rd consecutive month
- 🚀 Reduced feature implementation time by 55%

### Metrics
[Table of current vs target vs baseline]

### Challenges
- Template complexity for edge case X
- Team requested training on advanced SPARQL

### Next Month Goals
- Reach 85% coverage
- Complete migration of subsystem Y
- Onboard 2 new developers

### ROI Update
- Hours saved this month: 180
- Cumulative savings: 650 hours ($97,500 at $150/hour)
- Break-even: Achieved (month 5)
```

**Tools**:
- **Grafana**: Visualize metrics over time
- **GitHub Actions**: Automated metric collection
- **Google Sheets / Excel**: Manual tracking template
- **Custom scripts**: Parse CI logs, count entities, measure coverage

**Next**: Share metrics in monthly team meeting, adjust targets based on trends.
""" .

